//? Title: Recurrent Autoencoders: A Final State Must Remember the Image
//? Subtitle: Shared tanh cells, a repeated-code decoder, full backpropagation through time and MNIST controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A recurrent autoencoder reconstructs an ordered sequence from a compact final encoder state.
Here a MNIST image becomes twenty-eight rows of twenty-eight pixels. A width-32 tanh encoder
compresses them into its final state, and an independent decoder receives that same code at
every step before projecting each state into a sigmoid pixel row. This report checks the
recurrences by hand, derives full backpropagation through both halves, and runs the original
4,956-parameter model for ninety-six explicit Adam updates. Its held-out MSE 0.07988 is worse
than the fitting-mean baseline, and shifting codes barely changes the score. State histories,
input-gradient profiles and target-free decoding qualify what this short run demonstrates.
)
\toc

> One state carries the entire sequence

For row vectors x_t with F features, encoder state h_t and decoder state d_t with U units,

$$
h_0=0,\quad h_t=\tanh(x_tW_e+h_{t-1}R_e+b_e),\quad z=h_T,\\
d_0=0,\quad d_t=\tanh(zW_d+d_{t-1}R_d+b_d),\quad
\hat x_t=\sigma(d_tW_o+b_o),\qquad
L=\frac1{BTF}\sum_{b,t,j}(\hat x_{btj}-x_{btj})^2.
$$
\alttext(The encoder processes ordered rows and retains only its final state. A separate decoder repeatedly receives that state, starts at zero, and projects each recurrent state into a sigmoid output row. Mean squared error scores every pixel.)

The cells are simple tanh recurrences in the spirit of [Elman (1990)](https://doi.org/10.1207/s15516709cog1402_1).
Encoder and decoder weights differ but each set is shared over time. The decoder receives
neither target rows, previous target/output pixels, encoder histories nor attention. There is
no teacher forcing or state carried between observations/calls. This fixed-length repeated-code
architecture is an educational reconstruction model, not a language translation system.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/recurrent_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(54, 1100);
  d.node("x", {0, 0}, "Image as ordered rows\n28 steps × 28 pixels")
      .node("e", {6, 0}, "Shared tanh encoder\n28 states, width 32")
      .node("z", {12, 0}, "Only final state z\n32 continuous values")
      .node("r", {12, -3}, "Repeat z at every step\nZero decoder initial state")
      .node("d", {6, -6}, "Independent tanh decoder\nShared sigmoid row head")
      .node("l", {0, -6}, "Original row targets\nMean squared pixel error")
      .edge("x", "e")
      .edge("e", "z")
      .edge("z", "r")
      .edge("r", "d")
      .edge("d", "l")
      .edge("x", "l")
      .save_svg("build/recurrent_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/recurrent_method.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Only z crosses from encoder to decoder. Earlier encoder states remain available for diagnostics, but are not decoder inputs. The target branch ends at the objective.)
\alttext(An ordered image passes through a shared encoder and final code, then a repeated-code independent decoder. Clean rows are targets at the loss, with no target arrow into the decoder.)

The canonical `models::RecurrentEncode`, `RecurrentDecode` and `RecurrentAutoencoder` keep every
step differentiable. `ImageRowSequence` converts NHWC grayscale images or flat rows into ordered
sequences without resizing. The core performs no optimizer updates; complete fitting loops
remain in the report and native Python/R programs. Invalid dimensions, integer/nonfinite inputs
or mismatched store dtypes fail before new leaves are created. Partial encoder/decoder result
fields are explicitly undefined; populated tensor handles own their lifetimes.

> Three steps with one state value

Take T=3,F=U=1 and input [0.2,0.4,0.8]. Set encoder weights (W_e,R_e,b_e)=(0.5,0.25,0),
decoder weights (W_d,R_d,b_d)=(0.7,0.3,−0.1), and output weights (W_o,b_o)=(1.2,−0.2).
For instance h_1=tanh(0.1), h_2=tanh(0.2+0.25h_1), and the decoder starts at
\(d_1=\tanh(0.7h_3-0.1)\). Every decoder step uses h_3, not the corresponding input value.
The program compares every state and output with these scalar recurrences in Float64.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/recurrent_toy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n            = dm::nn;
  const auto        type = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto        x    = n::tensor({.2, .4, .8}, {1, 3, 1}, type);
  n::ParameterStore p(5, type);
  n::models::RecurrentAutoencoder(p, x, 3, 1, 1);
  p.unflatten({.5, .25, 0., .7, .3, -.1, 1.2, -.2});
  const auto          net = n::models::RecurrentAutoencoder(p, x, 3, 1, 1);
  double              h = 0, d = 0;
  std::vector<double> encoder, decoder, prediction, steps{1, 2, 3};
  for (double v : {.2, .4, .8}) {
    h = std::tanh(.5 * v + .25 * h);
    encoder.push_back(h);
  }
  std::print(
      "| step | input | encoder state | decoder state | output |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  for (int t = 0; t < 3; ++t) {
    d              = std::tanh(.7 * h + .3 * d - .1);
    const double y = 1 / (1 + std::exp(-(1.2 * d - .2)));
    decoder.push_back(d);
    prediction.push_back(y);
    if (std::abs(net.encoder_states.at({0, t, 0}) - encoder[t]) > 1e-14 ||
        std::abs(net.decoder_states.at({0, t, 0}) - d) > 1e-14 ||
        std::abs(net.reconstruction.at({0, t, 0}) - y) > 1e-14)
      return 1;
    std::print(
        "| {} | {:.1f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} |\n", t + 1, x.at({0, t, 0}), encoder[t], d, y);
  }
  if (n::max(
          n::abs(n::models::RecurrentDecode(p, net.code, 3, 1).reconstruction - net.reconstruction))
          .item() != 0)
    return 1;
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(steps, encoder, "encoder h", {37, 99, 235}, 2.)
      .lines(steps, decoder, "decoder d", {22, 163, 74}, 2.)
      .lines(steps, prediction, "sigmoid output", {220, 38, 38}, 2.)
      .x_label("time step")
      .y_label("scalar state / pixel prediction")
      .title("Three-step hand example: one final code drives every decoder step")
      .save_svg("build/recurrent_toy.svg");
}
```
