\caption(Encoder and decoder curves show their post-update states, excluding the zero initial states. The output curve is the shared sigmoid projection; its target is the three input values.)
\alttext(Three scalar trajectories over three steps show increasing encoder states, decoder states and sigmoid outputs, all verified against direct scalar calculations.)

> Full backpropagation through time

Define the output preactivation derivative
\(e_t=2(\hat x_t-x_t)\odot\hat x_t\odot(1-\hat x_t)/(BTF)\).
For each observation, the decoder preactivation adjoints, followed backwards, satisfy

$$
\delta^d_t=(e_tW_o^\top+\delta^d_{t+1}R_d^\top)\odot(1-d_t^2),\quad
\delta^d_{T+1}=0,\quad
 g_z=\sum_{t=1}^T\delta^d_tW_d^\top.
$$
\alttext(Each decoder step accumulates its local output gradient and recurrent gradient from the next step. Because the code is repeated, its gradient sums contributions from every decoder time.)

Then \(\delta^e_T=g_z\odot(1-h_T^2)\), and for t<T,
\(\delta^e_t=(\delta^e_{t+1}R_e^\top)\odot(1-h_t^2)\).
Shared-weight derivatives sum across both observations and time:

$$
\nabla W_e=\sum_tX_t^\top\delta^e_t,\quad
\nabla R_e=\sum_tH_{t-1}^\top\delta^e_t,\quad
\nabla W_d=\sum_tZ^\top\delta^d_t,\quad
\nabla R_d=\sum_tD_{t-1}^\top\delta^d_t,\quad
\nabla W_o=\sum_tD_t^\top e_t.
$$
\alttext(Each shared matrix accumulates its input transposed times the local preactivation derivative at every time step. Bias gradients sum the matching derivatives.)

There is no truncated history or detach at the code. Repeated Jacobian products can nevertheless
shrink or amplify early input sensitivities. The tanh slope is at most one; for a single example,
\(\|\partial h_T/\partial x_t\|\le\|W_e\|\|R_e\|^{T-t}\) in the spectral norm.
This bound concerns a fixed network, not a convergence guarantee. A strict final-state bottleneck
limits scalar count; continuous Float32 codes do not by themselves define a compressed bitstream.

> The executed 4,956-parameter architecture

With F=28,U=32, encoder parameters total FU+U²+U=1,952. Decoder recurrence contributes
2U²+U=2,080 and its shared row projection UF+F=924, giving 4,956 scalars in eight leaves.
The count is independent of T because weights are reused. Default work is
\(O(BT(FU+U^2))\), with \(O(BTU)\) recurrent histories plus the reconstructed pixels.
The block graph below executes all 28 steps in each half for one image, with named encoder and
decoder boundaries. It uses Graph style, Blocks detail, linear shape scales, a two-pixel
visibility floor, at most six sampled planes and eight sampled neurons. Exact shapes remain
in labels; unrolled time is summarized by the two labelled blocks rather than 56 repeated boxes.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/recurrent_architecture.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  n::ParameterStore p(1871);
  n::Module         model(
      [&](const n::Tensor& x) {
        const auto net = n::models::RecurrentAutoencoder(p, x);
        n::model_stage("Encoder: 28 shared tanh steps / final state", x, net.code);
        return n::model_stage(
            "Decoder: 28 repeated-code steps + sigmoid rows", net.code, net.reconstruction);
      },
      "Final-state-only recurrent autoencoder: shared width-32 cells");
  n::ModelPlotOptions options;
  options.style                 = n::ModelPlotStyle::Graph;
  options.detail                = n::ModelPlotDetail::Blocks;
  options.max_neurons           = 8;
  options.sizing.spatial_scale  = n::ModelPlotScale::Linear;
  options.sizing.channel_scale  = n::ModelPlotScale::Linear;
  options.sizing.minimum_extent = 2;
  options.sizing.max_planes     = 6;
  const auto figure             = model.plot(n::zeros({1, 28, 28}), options);
  if (p.size() != 8 || p.count() != 4956 || figure.parameter_count() != 4956)
    return 1;
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  figure.save_svg("build/recurrent_architecture.svg");
  std::print("| parameter | scalars |\n|---|---:|\n");
  for (const auto& name : p.names())
    std::print("| {} | {} |\n", name, p.at(name).numel());
}
