\alttext(Ten unfiltered digit columns compare clean images with learned reconstruction, constant mean, absolute error and three frozen controls.)

> Verification and limits

The C++ core is canonical for sequence conversion, shared recurrent components and independent
decoding; Python/R facades forward to the same functions and retain explicit experiment loops.
Native tests check a scalar hand example, all parameters and inputs of a small Float64 model by
finite differences, second derivatives, strict bottleneck validation, owning handles and the
original eight-leaf census. Saved arrays retain complete histories and the initial, first-update,
epoch and selected models for independent comparison. All source arrays in thirteen report archives agree by shape, dtype
and every value byte with the saved original. The same thirteen source archives agree across
normal C++, Python and R workflows. An optional NumPy oracle
(`scripts/check_recurrent_autoencoder.py`) checks all 4,956 first parameter derivatives
(maximum difference 2.0e-9), bit-exact first Adam updates, 1,724,800 prediction pixels
(maximum difference 1.1e-6 overall; 1.9e-7 at selected weights), 14,336 state-history values and all 6,272 fixed-target input
derivatives (maximum difference 1.7e-10). It does not prepare data or replace the optimizer loop. Full unrolling establishes a gradient
path through all time steps; it does not guarantee that a short optimizer run will learn an
adequate final-state representation. The mean and shifted-code controls make that limitation
visible in this particular experiment.

> References

- J. L. Elman (1990), [Finding Structure in Time](https://doi.org/10.1207/s15516709cog1402_1),
  Cognitive Science 14,179–211. Simple recurrent context motivates the cell, not this specific MNIST experiment.
- P. J. Werbos (1990), [Backpropagation through time: what it does and how to do it](https://doi.org/10.1109/5.58337),
  Proceedings of the IEEE 78(10),1550–1560. This implementation retains the full finite unroll.
- Y. LeCun, C. Cortes and C. J. C. Burges, [MNIST handwritten digit database](https://yann.lecun.com/exdb/mnist/).
  The native manifest records source files and selected official IDs.
